  "books": [
    {
      "id": "albers-interaction-of-color",
      "title": "配色の設計 — Interaction of Color",
      "author": "ジョセフ・アルバース",
      "publisher": "ビー・エヌ・エヌ新社",
      "year": 2016,
      "shelf": "theory",
      "accent": "#D9502B",
      "topics": [
        "wheel",
        "mix",
        "gradient",
        "perception",
        "warmcool",
        "huedist",
        "deltae",
        "dmatrix",
        "dbreak",
        "redundancy"
      ],
      "pitch": {
        "ja": "「色は隣り合う色で別の色に見える」。相互作用としての色を実習で体得する古典。",
        "en": "The classic on color as interaction — the same color reads as two when the neighbor changes."
      },
      "summary": {
        "ja": [
          "色は単独ではなく、隣り合う色との関係で見える。バウハウスからイェール大学へ渡ったジョセフ・アルバースが、講義で積み重ねた色の実習を1冊にまとめた古典。理論の解説よりも、実際に色紙を並べて「同じ色が違って見える」体験を積むことに主眼が置かれている。",
          "本ツールが数値で示す色差 ΔE や色相分布は、あくまで刺激としての色の量。それが人にどう見えるかは、隣接する色や面積比によって変わる。数値が同じでも印象が違う理由を、この本は実例で示してくれる。",
          "最初の1冊にも、数値に慣れたあとの「なぜ数値どおりに見えないのか」を埋める1冊にも向く。"
        ],
        "en": [
          "Color is almost never seen as it really is. Josef Albers distilled decades of teaching at the Bauhaus and Yale into a book built around exercises rather than theory: you place papers side by side and watch one color read as two.",
          "The delta-E and hue-distribution figures in this tool describe color as stimulus. How it is actually perceived depends on what sits beside it and in what proportion. This book demonstrates why identical numbers can leave different impressions.",
          "It works as a first book, and equally as the one that fills the gap once you are fluent in the numbers."
        ]
      },
